doc ///
  Key
    "using OSCAR"
  Headline
    how to use OSCAR from the Julia package
  Description
    Text
      In this tutorial, we will explore several different ways to use
      @HREF("https://oscar-system.org/", "OSCAR")@ from the Julia package.
      In particular, we will use it to compute a Gröbner basis for the
      ideal $(xy - 1, y^2 - 1)$ in the ring $\QQ[x,y]$.

      The simplest way to do this is to write pure Julia code and pass
      it to @TO juliaValue@.
    CannedExample
      i2 : juliaValue ////
           using Oscar
           R, (x,y) = QQ[:x,:y]
           I = ideal(R, [x*y - 1, y^2 - 1])
           groebner_basis(I)
           ////

      o2 = Gröbner basis with elements
             1: x - y
             2: y^2 - 1
           with respect to the ordering
             degrevlex([x, y])

      o2 : JuliaObject of type Oscar.IdealGens{QQMPolyRingElem}
    Text
      Another option is to pass one line of Julia code at a time to a
      @TO JuliaContext@ object.
    CannedExample
      i3 : oscar = JuliaContext "using Oscar"

      o3 = oscar

      o3 : JuliaContext

      i4 : oscar "R, (x,y) = QQ[:x,:y]"

      o4 = (Multivariate polynomial ring in 2 variables over QQ, QQMPolyRingElem[x, y])

      o4 : JuliaObject of type Tuple{QQMPolyRing, Vector{QQMPolyRingElem}}

      i5 : oscar "I = ideal(R, [x*y - 1, y^2 - 1])"

      o5 = Ideal generated by
             x*y - 1
             y^2 - 1

      o5 : JuliaObject of type MPolyIdeal{QQMPolyRingElem}

      i6 : oscar "groebner_basis(I)"

      o6 = Gröbner basis with elements
             1: x - y
             2: y^2 - 1
           with respect to the ordering
             degrevlex([x, y])

      o6 : JuliaObject of type Oscar.IdealGens{QQMPolyRingElem}
    Text
      Finally, we have the most granular control if we interact directly with
      with the @TO2(JuliaObject, "Julia objects")@ themselves.

      First, we load OSCAR with @TO using@.
    CannedExample
      i7 : using "Oscar"

      o7 = Oscar

      o7 : JuliaObject of type Module
    Text
      Next, we load the ring of rational numbers using @TO juliaGetGlobal@ and
      create several @TO JuliaFunction@ objects that we will need.
    CannedExample
      i8 : jlQQ = juliaGetGlobal "QQ"

      o8 = Rational field

      o8 : JuliaObject of type QQField

      i9 : jlPolynomialRing = JuliaFunction "polynomial_ring"

      o9 = polynomial_ring

      o9 : JuliaFunction

      i10 : jlIdeal = JuliaFunction "ideal"

      o10 = ideal

      o10 : JuliaFunction

      i11 : jlGroebnerBasis = JuliaFunction "groebner_basis"

      o11 = groebner_basis

      o11 : JuliaFunction
    Text
      Now, let's create our ring.  The @CODE "polynomial_ring"@ function returns
      a tuple containing the ring itself and a vector of its variables.  We can
      access each of these by subscripting with
      @TO (symbol _, JuliaObject, Thing)@.
    CannedExample
      i12 : Rxy = jlPolynomialRing(jlQQ, {"x", "y"})

      o12 = (Multivariate polynomial ring in 2 variables over QQ, QQMPolyRingElem[x, y])

      o12 : JuliaObject of type Tuple{QQMPolyRing, Vector{QQMPolyRingElem}}

      i13 : R = Rxy_1

      o13 = Multivariate polynomial ring in 2 variables x, y
              over rational field

      o13 : JuliaObject of type QQMPolyRing

      i14 : x = Rxy_2_1

      o14 = x

      o14 : JuliaObject of type QQMPolyRingElem

      i15 : y = Rxy_2_2

      o15 = y

      o15 : JuliaObject of type QQMPolyRingElem
    Text
      Now, we construct our ideal and compute its Gröbner basis.
    CannedExample
      i16 : I = jlIdeal(x*y - 1, y^2 - 1)

      o16 = Ideal generated by
              x*y - 1
              y^2 - 1

      o16 : JuliaObject of type MPolyIdeal{QQMPolyRingElem}

      i17 : G = jlGroebnerBasis I

      o17 = Gröbner basis with elements
              1: x - y
              2: y^2 - 1
            with respect to the ordering
              degrevlex([x, y])

      o17 : JuliaObject of type Oscar.IdealGens{QQMPolyRingElem}
    Text
      Notice that we can subscript the Gröbner basis object to access its
      elements.
    CannedExample
      i18 : G_1

      o18 = x - y

      o18 : JuliaObject of type QQMPolyRingElem

      i19 : G_2

      o19 = y^2 - 1

      o19 : JuliaObject of type QQMPolyRingElem
///

-- code for canned example:

-*

juliaValue ///
using Oscar
R, (x,y) = QQ[:x,:y]
I = ideal(R, [x*y - 1, y^2 - 1])
groebner_basis(I)
///

oscar = JuliaContext "using Oscar"
oscar "R, (x,y) = QQ[:x,:y]"
oscar "I = ideal(R, [x*y - 1, y^2 - 1])"
oscar "groebner_basis(I)"

using "Oscar"
jlQQ = juliaGetGlobal "QQ"
jlPolynomialRing = JuliaFunction "polynomial_ring"
jlIdeal = JuliaFunction "ideal"
jlGroebnerBasis = JuliaFunction "groebner_basis"

Rxy = jlPolynomialRing(jlQQ, {"x", "y"})
R = Rxy_1
x = Rxy_2_1
y = Rxy_2_2
I = jlIdeal(x*y - 1, y^2 - 1)
G = jlGroebnerBasis I
G_1
G_2

*-
